(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-3-copd-smoking-cessation-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease, Smoking Cessation & Immunizations Quiz 2 — Pharmacology I Exam 3","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which factor directly correlates with a patient's success in quitting smoking?","choices":["Using medication without counseling","Daily cigarette count at the start","Length of the smoking history","Intensity and frequency of counseling"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Effective programs combine pharmacologic intervention with counseling, and the intensity and frequency of counseling directly correlate with the success of quitting. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 103"},{"q":"What is the purpose of the nicotine replacement agents?","choices":["To reduce craving and physical withdrawal","To inhibit norepinephrine and dopamine uptake","To partly block the nicotine receptor","To make cigarettes taste unpleasant"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Nicotine replacement reduces the craving to smoke and minimizes physical withdrawal (anxiety, irritability, restlessness, insomnia, headache, depression and nausea). — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 106"},{"q":"Why is annual influenza vaccination highly recommended for patients with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease?","choices":["Influenza triggers most asthma attacks","The vaccine slows the fall in lung function","It replaces the pneumococcal vaccine","They risk serious pneumonia from influenza"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Patients with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease are at risk for significant morbidity and mortality from pneumonia caused by influenza, so annual vaccination is highly recommended. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 108"},{"q":"A patient with a seizure disorder asks for help to stop smoking. Which agent should be avoided?","choices":["Varenicline","Bupropion","Nicotine gum","Nicotine patch"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Bupropion sustained release is to be avoided in patients with seizure disorders and in patients taking monoamine oxidase inhibitors. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 105"},{"q":"A patient with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has mild symptoms but had two exacerbations last year, one needing hospitalization. What is the suggested regular treatment?","choices":["A regular long-acting beta-2 agonist","A short-acting bronchodilator as needed","A regular long-acting muscarinic antagonist","An inhaled glucocorticoid with a long-acting beta-2 agonist"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Group C (less symptomatic, high risk: two or more exacerbations a year with one or more hospitalized) gets regular treatment with a long-acting muscarinic antagonist, with a short-acting beta-2 agonist for relief. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 102"},{"q":"A patient with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease is breathless only with strenuous exercise and had no exacerbations last year. What is the suggested treatment?","choices":["A short-acting bronchodilator as needed","A regular long-acting bronchodilator","A regular long-acting muscarinic antagonist","A long-acting beta-2 agonist plus muscarinic antagonist"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Group A (mild or infrequent symptoms, 0 or 1 exacerbation without hospitalization) is treated with a short-acting bronchodilator, beta-2 agonist, antimuscarinic or both, as needed. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 102"},{"q":"Which smoking cessation agent is marketed as Zyban?","choices":["Varenicline","Nicotine polacrilex","Bupropion","Nicotine transdermal system"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Bupropion sustained release (Zyban) is one of the six listed smoking cessation agents, with the nicotine patch, gum, spray and inhaler, and varenicline (Chantix). — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"Which finding places a patient with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease in a high-risk treatment group?","choices":["Two exacerbations a year, one hospitalized","Breathlessness walking on level ground","Chronic hypoxemia needing oxygen","One exacerbation without hospitalization"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. High risk (groups C and D) is two or more exacerbations per year with one or more leading to hospitalization; 0 or 1 exacerbation without hospitalization is low risk. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 102"},{"q":"What is the usual duration of nicotine replacement therapy?","choices":["2 to 4 weeks","6 to 12 weeks","16 to 20 weeks","24 to 52 weeks"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The duration of nicotine replacement therapy is between 6 and 12 weeks, set by the level of dependence and point in therapy. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 106"},{"q":"Which side effects are listed for varenicline?","choices":["Nausea, sleep disturbance and headache","Tachyarrhythmias and hypertension","Orange tears, sweat and urine","Oral thrush and growth delay"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Varenicline's listed side effects are nausea, sleep disturbance and headache, and it carries a black box warning for neuropsychiatric events. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 107"},{"q":"Which measure is recommended for every patient with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, whatever the treatment group?","choices":["A regular long-acting bronchodilator","An inhaled glucocorticoid","A long-acting muscarinic antagonist","Pneumococcal vaccination"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Measures for all groups are avoiding risk factors such as smoking, annual influenza and pneumococcal vaccination, regular physical activity, inhaler technique review, long-term oxygen if chronically hypoxemic and pulmonary rehabilitation. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 102"},{"q":"When should varenicline be started relative to the quit date?","choices":["On the quit date itself","One week after the quit date","One week before the quit date","Once withdrawal symptoms begin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Varenicline is started one week before the quit date and continued for 12 weeks. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 107"},{"q":"In which patient should bupropion be avoided rather than used with caution?","choices":["One taking a tricyclic antidepressant","One with cardiovascular disease","One taking a monoamine oxidase inhibitor","One with a history of depression"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Bupropion is avoided in patients taking monoamine oxidase inhibitors and in seizure disorders; noradrenergic antidepressants (tricyclics and venlafaxine) call only for caution. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 105"},{"q":"Which feature of the inflammation in chronic obstructive pulmonary disease distinguishes it from asthma?","choices":["It causes widespread reversible narrowing","It is generally not fully reversible","It is eosinophilic in the bronchial mucosa","It spares the lung parenchyma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease is chronic inflammation of the airways and lung parenchyma that is generally not fully reversible, with an accelerated decline in lung function. — Pulmonology-2.pptx, Slide 101"}]};
